4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333108} {:answer/id #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333155} {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333119} {:answer/id #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334369} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} {:answer/id #uuid "af99c88e-1b1e-54e6-8e94-cc6dcf9705d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{9}" :db/